<Base lang="en" title="EWS Certificate UP: Eligibility, Documents & Application" description="UP EWS Income and Asset Certificate guide covering the ₹8 lakh income test, property limits, family definition, documents and application." path="/en/ews-certificate/" altPath="/ews-certificate/" faqs={faqs} updated="23 October 2026">
<div class="content-col"><h1 class="page-title">EWS Income & Asset Certificate — Uttar Pradesh</h1><p class="updated-line">Understand both the income and property tests before applying</p>
<div class="warn"><strong>Read the notice first:</strong> A recruitment, admission or scheme can prescribe its certificate format, authority and issue period. That current notice controls acceptance.</div>
<div class="action-grid"><a class="action-card" href="https://esathi.up.gov.in/citizenservices/login/login.aspx" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><strong>Official citizen portal ↗</strong><span>Check the available service and current requirements</span></a><a class="action-card" href="/en/income-certificate/"><strong>Income evidence guide</strong><span>Prepare family-income records</span></a></div>
<section class="box"><h2>EWS certificate versus an income certificate</h2><p>An ordinary income certificate records verified income. An EWS Income and Asset Certificate additionally addresses whether the applicant is outside the relevant reserved categories and whether the family remains within prescribed property limits. An income certificate alone therefore does not establish an EWS reservation claim.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Central eligibility snapshot</h2><ul class="checklist"><li>The applicant is not covered by SC, ST or OBC reservation.</li><li>Gross annual family income is below ₹8 lakh.</li><li>The family does not own 5 acres or more of agricultural land.</li><li>The family does not own a residential flat of 1,000 sq ft or more.</li><li>The family does not own a residential plot of 100 sq yards or more in a notified municipality.</li><li>The family does not own a residential plot of 200 sq yards or more outside a notified municipality.</li></ul><p>Central DoPT instructions aggregate property held in different locations. Crossing one asset threshold can affect eligibility even when income is below the ceiling.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Who counts as family?</h2><p>The central framework includes the applicant, parents, siblings below 18, spouse and children below 18. Do not examine only the applicant's salary. Follow the exact family wording in the prescribed form and recruitment notice, especially for married applicants or major children.</p><div class="note">Do not confuse assessment year, financial year and issue date. Use records for the period requested by the accepting notice.</div></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Eligibility self-check</h2><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>If yes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Covered by SC/ST/OBC reservation?</td><td>Central EWS reservation generally does not apply.</td></tr><tr><td>Gross family income ₹8 lakh or more?</td><td>The income test is not met.</td></tr><tr><td>Any listed asset threshold met/exceeded?</td><td>An asset exclusion can apply.</td></tr><tr><td>Notice requires a current prescribed format?</td><td>An old or generic certificate may be rejected.</td></tr></tbody></table></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Document preparation</h2><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Possible records</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Identity/address</td><td>Aadhaar, voter ID, ration card or proof accepted by the authority</td></tr><tr><td>Salary/pension</td><td>Salary slips, employer certificate, Form 16, pension statement</td></tr><tr><td>Business/profession</td><td>ITR, accounts or applicable income records</td></tr><tr><td>Agriculture</td><td>Khatauni/land records and agricultural-income details</td></tr><tr><td>Property</td><td>Flat/plot documents, area and municipal classification</td></tr><tr><td>Family/declaration</td><td>Family record and the prescribed signed declaration</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="warn">Do not assume one universal 100 KB or JPEG-only rule. Follow the current format and size shown for each upload field.</div></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Application sequence</h2><ol><li>Register/sign in on the official citizen portal and locate the EWS/Income &amp; Asset service.</li><li>Read the current authority, fee and document instructions.</li><li>Declare all family members, income sources and land/flat/plot holdings accurately.</li><li>Upload readable files in the field-specific size and format.</li><li>Review spellings, financial year, area units and property locations.</li><li>Pay only the amount displayed and preserve the receipt/application number.</li><li>Use the <a href="/en/status-verification/">status guide</a> to track and verify the issued result.</li></ol></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Common rejection risks</h2><ul><li>Leaving out business, agricultural, pension or other family income.</li><li>Confusing square feet and square yards.</li><li>Not declaring family property in another district or state.</li><li>Using evidence for the wrong financial year.</li><li>Name/address mismatch or unreadable land records.</li><li>Submitting a certificate in a format or from an authority not accepted by the notice.</li></ul><p>Read the rejection remark before filing again. A false declaration can create serious problems during later verification; ask the issuing authority when uncertain.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Official basis and current-data rule</h2><p>The ₹8 lakh income ceiling and the 5-acre, 1,000-sq-ft and 100/200-sq-yard thresholds come from Government of India DoPT EWS instructions. For a UP application, follow the current portal service screen and state-prescribed format. Verify the fee, file limits and processing period on the submission date rather than treating guide figures as fixed.</p></section></div></Base>
